-- complier's pattern library: multi-agent topologies as CSP processes.
--
-- A spec with roles loads it with
--
--   include "patterns.csp"
--
-- complier-fdr supplies this file (it's built into it), so the include needs no copy next to the
-- spec. Every pattern is an ordinary process over the prelude's channels, so FDR proves a policy
-- built from them like any other.
--
-- The patterns take two kinds of argument:
--
--   a phase      a process that ends (SKIP) when its part of the work is done, e.g.
--                  Draft = Permit(writer, edit, \ _ @ Draft) [] Step(writer, submit)
--   a judgement  a function of two processes, J(Pass, Fail): it runs, then carries on as one of
--                them, e.g. Judgement(critic, review, {approved})

-- One call by role r, then the phase ends, whatever the call returned.
Step(r, x) = Permit(r, x, \ _ @ SKIP)

-- Role r's call x is the judgement: a result in Passing passes, and any other result (a failed call
-- too) fails.
Judgement(r, x, Passing) = \ Pass, Fail @ Permit(r, x, \ v @ if member(v, Passing) then Pass else Fail)

-- Sequential pipeline: the phases run in order, each starting once the one before it has ended.
Pipeline(<>) = SKIP
Pipeline(<P>^Ps) = P ; Pipeline(Ps)

-- Coordinator/dispatcher: Route is the coordinator's turn. Route(Handle) either hands the request to
-- a specialist, by carrying on as Handle(d), or ends. Specialist(d) is that specialist's phase; once
-- it ends, the coordinator routes the next request.
Dispatcher(Route, Specialist) = Route(\ d @ Specialist(d) ; Dispatcher(Route, Specialist))

-- Parallel fan-out/gather: the branches run at the same time, and Gather starts once every one of
-- them has ended. Each branch is its own agents: branches that share a role's calls make the policy
-- nondeterministic, and FDR refuses it.
FanOut(Branches, Gather) = Together(Branches) ; Gather
Together(<>) = SKIP
Together(<B>^Bs) = B ||| Together(Bs)

-- Generator/critic: the generator's phase, then the critic's judgement. A pass ends the pattern; a
-- fail sends the work back to the generator.
GeneratorCritic(Generate, Critic) = Generate ; Critic(SKIP, GeneratorCritic(Generate, Critic))

-- Iterative refinement: the generator's phase, then at most n rounds of critique. A pass ends it
-- early; otherwise the refiner's phase runs and the next round starts. After n refinements it ends.
Refinement(n, Generate, Critic, Refine) = Generate ; Rounds(n, Critic, Refine)
Rounds(0, _, _) = SKIP
Rounds(n, Critic, Refine) = Critic(SKIP, Refine ; Rounds(n - 1, Critic, Refine))

-- A held call: role r's call x is taken, and answered only once H has ended. Until then r waits (its
-- tool call doesn't return) while H's agents act. Then the call is allowed, and its result v carries
-- on as K(v).
Hold(r, x, H, K) = attempt.r.x -> (H ; allow.r.x -> result.r.x?v : Returns(x) -> K(v))

-- Hierarchical decomposition: r's call x hands a task to Sub, the agents below it, and is held until
-- Sub ends. Sub may delegate in turn.
Delegate(r, x, Sub, K) = Hold(r, x, Sub, K)

-- Advisor: r's call x asks advisor a, and is held until a answers with one call y; then r's call
-- runs (and can read the answer). The advisor acts only when asked, and only to answer.
Consult(r, x, a, y, K) = Hold(r, x, Step(a, y), K)

-- Guard: r's call x is put to Judge before it runs. If the judge passes it, it's allowed, and its
-- result v carries on as K(v); if not, it's denied, and the policy carries on as P. r never calls
-- the judge: its attempt is what wakes it.
Guard(r, x, Judge, K, P) = attempt.r.x -> Judge(allow.r.x -> result.r.x?v : Returns(x) -> K(v), deny.r.x -> P)
